</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The x y plane showing the setup for the definition of the directional derivative. From the point P 0 with coordinates x 0 comma y 0, a red arrow shows the unit vector u, and a longer blue arrow shows the displacement s u, ending at the point P with coordinates x 0 plus s u 1 comma y 0 plus s u 2. Dashed gray segments show the horizontal and vertical components of the displacement.">
  <annotation ref="ds-P0" text="The base point P 0 with coordinates x 0 comma y 0, where the directional derivative is computed."/>
  <annotation ref="ds-u" text="The unit vector u equals u 1 i plus u 2 j, drawn in red, giving the direction of differentiation."/>
  <annotation ref="ds-su" text="The displacement vector s u, drawn in blue, which moves the distance s from P 0 in the direction u."/>
  <annotation ref="ds-comp1" text="The horizontal component of the displacement, s u 1 i, drawn as a dashed segment."/>
  <annotation ref="ds-comp2" text="The vertical component of the displacement, s u 2 j, drawn as a dashed segment."/>
  <annotation ref="ds-P" text="The point P with coordinates x 0 plus s u 1 comma y 0 plus s u 2, which approaches P 0 as s goes to 0."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-dirderiv-setup-video">
<caption>
Animation of the definition of the directional derivative. The vertical
plane through <m>P_0</m> in the direction <m>\hat{u}</m> cuts the
surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> in a curve, and the point
<m>P = P_0 + s\hat{u}</m> moves on this plane. The secant line through
<m>(P_0, f(P_0))</m> and <m>(P, f(P))</m> has slope
<m>\big(f(P) - f(P_0)\big)/s</m>, and as <m>s \to 0</m> it rotates onto
the tangent line, whose slope is the directional derivative
<m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-dirderiv-setup"
       source="videos/dirderiv_secant_tangent_manim.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/dirderiv_secant_tangent_manim.jpg"
       width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-int-dirderiv-definition">
<caption>
The difference quotient <m>\big(f(P)-f(P_0)\big)/s</m> for <m>f(x,y)=1+x^2+y^2</m> at <m>P_0(1,1)</m>, as functions of the direction angle and the step <m>s</m>.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-dirderiv-definition" iframe="dirderiv-definition.html" width="100%" aspect="7:6">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the slider for the angle to choose the unit vector <m>\hat{u}</m>,
and drag the slider for <m>s</m> to move the point <m>P</m> along the
direction <m>\hat{u}</m> starting at <m>P_0(1,1)</m>, for the function
<m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>. The readout compares the difference
quotient <m>\big(f(P) - f(P_0)\big)/s</m> with its limit as
<m>s \to 0</m>, which is the directional derivative
<m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m>.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<p>
Before developing any shortcuts, we evaluate a directional derivative straight
from <xref ref="def-directional-derivative"/>. Working through the limit once
makes the definition concrete, and it gives us a value to check against later:
<xref ref="example-dirderiv-gradient"/> recovers the same number in a couple of
lines, once the gradient is available.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-dirderiv-definition">
<title>Example I</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>, the point
<m>P_0(1,1)</m>, and the unit vector
<m>\hat{u} = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\imath} + \dfrac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\jmath}</m>.
Use the definition of directional derivative to compute
<m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m>.
</p>
</statement>


<solution>
<p>
We substitute <m>x_0 = y_0 = 1</m> and <m>u_1 = u_2 = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt 2}</m>
in the definition:
<md>
<mrow>
\left(\frac{df}{ds}\right)_{\hat{u},P_0}
\amp= \lim_{s\to 0}\frac{f(x_0 + su_1,\, y_0 + su_2) - f(x_0,y_0)}{s}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \lim_{s\to 0}
\frac{f\!\left(1 + \tfrac{s}{\sqrt 2},\, 1 + \tfrac{s}{\sqrt 2}\right) - f(1,1)}{s}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \lim_{s\to 0}
\frac{1 + \left(1 + \tfrac{s}{\sqrt 2}\right)^2
+ \left(1 + \tfrac{s}{\sqrt 2}\right)^2 - 3}{s}
</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \lim_{s\to 0}\frac{\tfrac{4s}{\sqrt 2} + s^2}{s} = \lim_{s\to 0}\left(\tfrac{4}{\sqrt 2} + s\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= 2\sqrt 2.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
The computation has a useful geometric picture, illustrated in the
interactive 3D figure below. Along the ray through
<m>P_0(1,1)</m> in the direction <m>\hat{u}</m>, the values of <m>f</m>
are the single-variable function
<md>
<mrow>g(s) \amp= f\!\left(1 + \tfrac{s}{\sqrt 2},\, 1 + \tfrac{s}{\sqrt 2}\right)</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-dirderiv-along-ray" number="yes">\amp= 3 + 2\sqrt 2\, s + s^2,</mrow>
</md>
and the difference quotient in the limit above is the slope of the
secant line of <m>g</m> through <m>s = 0</m> and <m>s</m>. As
<m>s \to 0</m> the secant lines approach the tangent line at
<m>s = 0</m>, whose slope is
<m>g'(0) = 2\sqrt 2 = \left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m>.
</p>
<!-- Hidden: superseded by fig-int-dirderiv-secant-3d-manim below, which shows
     the same limit. Uncomment to bring it back.
<figure xml:id="fig-int-dirderiv-secant-3d">
<caption>
The secant line on the surface <m>z=1+x^2+y^2</m> rotating onto the tangent line as <m>s \to 0</m>, giving the directional derivative <m>2\sqrt2</m>.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-dirderiv-secant-3d" iframe="dirderiv-secant-3d.html" width="100%" aspect="8:6">
<instructions>
<p>
The surface <m>z = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> is cut by the vertical plane through
<m>P_0(1,1)</m> in the direction <m>\hat{u}</m>. Drag the slider to
change <m>s</m>: the point <m>P</m> slides along <m>\hat{u}</m> in the
<m>xy</m>-plane, and the secant line through the surface points
<m>(1,\,1,\,f(P_0))</m> and <m>(1+su_1,\,1+su_2,\,f(P))</m> rotates
toward the tangent line. Press <q>s <m>\to</m> 0</q> to animate the
limit; the readout shows the secant slope <m>2\sqrt 2 + s</m>
approaching the directional derivative <m>2\sqrt 2</m>. Drag the figure
to view it from a different angle, or press the <q>Rotate</q> button.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
-->

<figure xml:id="fig-int-dirderiv-secant-3d-manim">
<caption>
The secant through <m>(P_0, f(P_0))</m> and <m>(P, f(P))</m> on the
surface <m>z = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>, rotating onto the tangent line at
<m>P_0</m> as <m>s \to 0</m>, with slope
<m>2\sqrt2 + s \to 2\sqrt2</m>.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-dirderiv-secant-3d-manim" iframe="dirderiv-secant-3d-manim.html" width="100%" aspect="1:1">
<instructions>
<p>
Press <q>Play</q> to watch the whole construction in one continuous
motion: the vertical plane through <m>P_0(1,1)</m> in the direction
<m>\hat{u}</m> cuts the surface <m>z = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> in the gold
curve, the red secant joins <m>(P_0, f(P_0))</m> to <m>(P, f(P))</m>,
and as <m>s \to 0</m> that secant rotates onto the green tangent line.
The reading at the lower left compares the secant slope
<m>2\sqrt 2 + s</m> with the tangent slope <m>2\sqrt 2</m>, which is the
directional derivative <m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m>. Stop
the animation at any time and drag the <m>s</m> slider yourself, or
press <q>s <m>\to</m> 0</q> to replay just the limit. Drag the figure to
change the viewpoint, or press <q>Spin</q> to let it turn.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>


<activity xml:id="activity-dirderiv-partials">
<title>The Partial Derivatives Are Directional Derivatives</title>

<introduction>
<p>
<xref ref="def-directional-derivative"/> measures the rate of change of <m>f</m> in
an arbitrary direction <m>\hat{u}</m>. Two directions are already familiar from
<m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m>. In this activity you will check that the definition
reproduces exactly those two derivatives when <m>\hat{u}</m> points along a
coordinate axis. Use only <xref ref="eq-dirderiv-definition"/>; nothing beyond the
definition is needed.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="task-dirderiv-i">
<statement>
<p>
Take <m>\hat{u} = \hat{\imath}</m>, so that <m>u_1 = 1</m> and <m>u_2 = 0</m>.
Substitute these into <xref ref="eq-dirderiv-definition"/> and simplify. Which
single-variable limit is left, and what is its value?
</p>
</statement>
<hint>
<p>
Ask yourself which of the two inputs of <m>f</m> actually changes as <m>s</m> varies.
</p>
</hint>
<answer>
<p>
Substituting <m>u_1 = 1</m> and <m>u_2 = 0</m> leaves the second slot of <m>f</m>
fixed at <m>y_0</m>:
<md>
<mrow>
\left(\frac{df}{ds}\right)_{\hat{\imath},\,P_0}
\amp= \lim_{s\to 0}\frac{f(x_0 + s\cdot 1,\; y_0 + s\cdot 0) - f(x_0,y_0)}{s}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \lim_{s\to 0}\frac{f(x_0 + s,\; y_0) - f(x_0,y_0)}{s}
= f_x(x_0,y_0).
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Only the first variable moves, so this is precisely the limit that defines the partial
derivative with respect to <m>x</m>. That is,
<m>\left(D_{\hat{\imath}} f\right)_{P_0} = f_x(x_0,y_0)</m>.
</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-dirderiv-j">
<statement>
<p>
Repeat the computation with <m>\hat{u} = \hat{\jmath}</m>, and state the conclusion.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
Now <m>u_1 = 0</m> and <m>u_2 = 1</m>, so the first slot is held at <m>x_0</m>:
<md>
<mrow>
\left(\frac{df}{ds}\right)_{\hat{\jmath},\,P_0}
\amp= \lim_{s\to 0}\frac{f(x_0,\; y_0 + s) - f(x_0,y_0)}{s}
= f_y(x_0,y_0),
</mrow>
</md>
so <m>\left(D_{\hat{\jmath}} f\right)_{P_0} = f_y(x_0,y_0)</m>. The partial derivatives
are the directional derivatives in the two coordinate directions, and
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f</m> is the common generalization of both.
</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-dirderiv-check">
<statement>
<p>
Test this on the function of <xref ref="example-dirderiv-definition"/>. With
<m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> and <m>P_0(1,1)</m>, compute
<m>\left(D_{\hat{\imath}} f\right)_{P_0}</m> straight from the definition, then compute
<m>f_x(1,1)</m> in the usual way and compare.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
From the definition,
<md>
<mrow>
\left(D_{\hat{\imath}} f\right)_{P_0}
\amp= \lim_{s\to 0}\frac{f(1+s,\,1) - f(1,1)}{s}
= \lim_{s\to 0}\frac{\left[1 + (1+s)^2 + 1\right] - 3}{s}
</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \lim_{s\to 0}\frac{2s + s^2}{s} = \lim_{s\to 0}\,(2 + s)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= 2 .</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Differentiating instead, <m>f_x = 2x</m>, so <m>f_x(1,1) = 2</m>. The two agree. By
symmetry <m>\left(D_{\hat{\jmath}} f\right)_{P_0} = f_y(1,1) = 2</m> as well. Note that
both are smaller than the value <m>2\sqrt 2</m> found in
<xref ref="example-dirderiv-definition"/>: the function climbs faster along the
diagonal than along either axis.
</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-dirderiv-negative">
<statement>
<p>
Finally, take <m>\hat{u} = -\hat{\imath}</m>. Show that
<m>\left(D_{-\hat{\imath}} f\right)_{P_0} = -f_x(x_0,y_0)</m>, and say in a sentence why
this is what you should expect.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
Here <m>u_1 = -1</m> and <m>u_2 = 0</m>, so
<md>
<mrow>
\left(D_{-\hat{\imath}} f\right)_{P_0}
\amp= \lim_{s\to 0}\frac{f(x_0 - s,\; y_0) - f(x_0,y_0)}{s} .
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Putting <m>h = -s</m> turns this into
<m>-\lim_{h\to 0}\dfrac{f(x_0+h,\,y_0) - f(x_0,y_0)}{h} = -f_x(x_0,y_0)</m>.
Walking the opposite way along the same line makes <m>f</m> change at the same rate
with the opposite sign, so reversing <m>\hat{u}</m> reverses the sign of
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f</m>.
</p>
</answer>
</task>

<conclusion>
<p>
Every partial derivative is a directional derivative, so nothing already known about
<m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m> is lost. What the definition adds is the freedom to point
<m>\hat{u}</m> anywhere. Keep in mind that this depends on <m>\hat{u}</m> being a
<em>unit</em> vector: the parameter <m>s</m> measures actual distance travelled in the
<m>xy</m>-plane only when <m>\|\hat{u}\| = 1</m>.
</p>
</conclusion>

</activity>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-gradient-vector">
<title>The Gradient Vector</title>

<p>
Note that we can write the equation of the line through the point
<m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> with the direction
<m>\hat{u} = u_1\hat{\imath} + u_2\hat{\jmath}</m> as
<md xml:id="eq-line-through-P0" number="yes">
x(s) = x_0 + su_1, \qquad y(s) = y_0 + su_2.
</md>
</p>

<p>
We can use the above relation between <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and <m>s</m>
and the chain rule, whose dependency diagram is shown in
<xref ref="fig-chain-rule-tree-prefigure"/>, to compute the directional
derivative as follows:
<md>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0} = \left(\frac{df}{ds}\right)_{\hat{u},P_0} \amp= \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)_{P_0}\frac{dx}{ds}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\quad + \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)_{P_0}\frac{dy}{ds}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)_{P_0}\cdot u_1</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\quad + \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)_{P_0}\cdot u_2</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \left[\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)_{P_0}\hat{\imath}
+ \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)_{P_0}\hat{\jmath}\right]
\cdot \left(u_1\hat{\imath} + u_2\hat{\jmath}\right).
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
The vector in the first set of parentheses is known as the
<term>gradient vector</term>.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-gradient-vector">
<title>Gradient Vector</title>
<statement>
<p>
The <term>gradient vector</term> of the function <m>f(x,y)</m> at
<m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> is the vector
<md xml:id="eq-gradient-definition" number="yes">
\left(\nabla f\right)_{P_0}
= \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)_{P_0}\hat{\imath}
+ \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)_{P_0}\hat{\jmath}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
The gradient vector and the directional derivative of <m>f(x,y)</m> at
<m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> in the direction
<m>\hat{u} = u_1\hat{\imath} + u_2\hat{\jmath}</m> are related via the
following dot product:
<md xml:id="eq-dirderiv-gradient-relation" number="yes">
\left(\frac{df}{ds}\right)_{\hat{u},\,P_0}
= \left(\nabla f\right)_{P_0}\cdot\hat{u}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<figure xml:id="fig-chain-rule-tree-prefigure">
<caption>
The chain rule dependency diagram: multiplying down each branch and
adding the two products gives
<m>\frac{df}{ds} = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{ds}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{ds}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="48%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-chain-rule-tree">
<diagram dimensions="(300,280)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(-3.0,-0.7,3.0,3.7)">
<label at="ct-z" anchor="(0,3.1)" alignment="c" scale="1.4">
<m>z=f(x,y)</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-x" anchor="(-1.7,1.5)" alignment="c" scale="1.4">
<m>x</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-y" anchor="(1.7,1.5)" alignment="c" scale="1.4">
<m>y</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-s" anchor="(0,-0.1)" alignment="c" scale="1.4">
<m>s</m>
</label>
<vector at="ct-zx" v="(-1.1,-1.05)" tail="(-0.45,2.85)" stroke="#0072B2"
        thickness="2"/>
<vector at="ct-zy" v="(1.1,-1.05)" tail="(0.45,2.85)" stroke="#0072B2"
        thickness="2"/>
<vector at="ct-xs" v="(1.25,-1.05)" tail="(-1.5,1.25)" stroke="#D55E00"
        thickness="2"/>
<vector at="ct-ys" v="(-1.25,-1.05)" tail="(1.5,1.25)" stroke="#D55E00"
        thickness="2"/>
<label at="ct-dfdx" anchor="(-1.75,2.55)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2" scale="1.4">
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-dfdy" anchor="(1.75,2.55)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2" scale="1.4">
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-dxds" anchor="(-1.6,0.55)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00" scale="1.4">
<m>\dfrac{dx}{ds}</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-dyds" anchor="(1.6,0.55)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00" scale="1.4">
<m>\dfrac{dy}{ds}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A dependency tree for the chain rule. At the top is z equals f of x comma y. Blue arrows labeled partial f partial x and partial f partial y lead down to the intermediate variables x and y. Red arrows labeled d x d s and d y d s lead from x and y down to the single independent variable s.">
  <annotation ref="ct-z" text="The dependent variable z equals f of x comma y at the top of the tree."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-zx" text="The branch from z to x, labeled with the partial derivative of f with respect to x."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-zy" text="The branch from z to y, labeled with the partial derivative of f with respect to y."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-xs" text="The branch from x down to s, labeled d x d s, which equals u 1 along the line through P 0."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-ys" text="The branch from y down to s, labeled d y d s, which equals u 2 along the line through P 0."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-s" text="The independent variable s, the arc length along the line through P 0 in the direction u."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
A fundamental geometric property of the gradient is that, at every point,
it is perpendicular to the level curve of <m>f</m> passing through that
point, as the interactive figure below lets you verify. We state and
prove this fact now.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-gradient-perp-level-curve">
<title>The Gradient is Perpendicular to the Level Curves</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>f(x,y)</m> be a differentiable function, and let <m>C</m> be the
level curve <m>f(x,y) = c</m> for a constant <m>c</m>. At every point of
<m>C</m> where <m>\nabla f \neq \mathbf 0</m>, the gradient vector
<m>\nabla f</m> is perpendicular to the level curve <m>C</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>
Suppose the level curve <m>C</m> is parametrized by a differentiable
vector function
<md xml:id="eq-level-curve-param" number="yes">
\mathbf r(t) = \langle x(t),\, y(t)\rangle,
</md>
and let <m>\mathbf v(t) = \langle x'(t),\, y'(t)\rangle</m> denote its
velocity vector. Because every point of <m>C</m> lies on the level curve,
its coordinates satisfy
<md xml:id="eq-level-curve-constant" number="yes">
f\bigl(x(t),\, y(t)\bigr) = c \qquad \text{for all } t.
</md>
</p>

<p>
The left-hand side is constant in <m>t</m>, so its derivative is zero.
Differentiating both sides with respect to <m>t</m> and applying the
chain rule gives
<md>
<mrow>\frac{d}{dt}\, f\bigl(x(t), y(t)\bigr) \amp= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\,\frac{dx}{dt} + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\,\frac{dy}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-level-curve-chain-rule" number="yes">\amp= 0.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
We recognize the middle expression as the dot product of the gradient
vector with the velocity vector:
<md>
<mrow>\left\langle \frac{\partial f}{\partial x},\, \frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \right\rangle \cdot \left\langle \frac{dx}{dt},\, \frac{dy}{dt} \right\rangle \amp= \nabla f \cdot \mathbf v(t)</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-level-curve-dot-product" number="yes">\amp= 0.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Therefore, at each point of <m>C</m>, the gradient <m>\nabla f</m> is
orthogonal to the velocity vector <m>\mathbf v(t)</m>, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-gradient-perp-prefigure"/>. Since the velocity vector
points in the direction of motion along the curve, it is tangent to
<m>C</m>; hence <m>\nabla f</m> being orthogonal to <m>\mathbf v(t)</m>
means precisely that <m>\nabla f</m> is perpendicular to the level curve
<m>C</m>. This holds at every point where <m>\nabla f \neq \mathbf 0</m>.
</p>
</proof>
</theorem>

<figure xml:id="fig-gradient-perp-prefigure">
<caption>
The gradient <m>\nabla f</m> meets the level curve <m>C\colon f = c</m>
at a right angle: it is perpendicular to the velocity vector
<m>\mathbf v(t)</m>, which is tangent to <m>C</m>.
</caption>
<image width="58%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-gradient-perp">
<diagram dimensions="(360,320)" margins="10">
<definition>ce(t) = (2.4*cos(t), 1.6*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>ci(t) = (1.63*cos(t), 1.09*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>co(t) = (3.12*cos(t), 2.08*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.7,-2.7,3.7,2.9)">
<parametric-curve at="gp-inner" function="ci" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F"/>
<parametric-curve at="gp-outer" function="co" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F"/>
<parametric-curve at="gp-C" function="ce" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="gp-ra1" endpoints="((1.470,1.510),(1.271,1.603))"
      stroke="black" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="gp-ra2" endpoints="((1.271,1.603),(1.178,1.404))"
      stroke="black" thickness="1.5"/>
<vector at="gp-grad" v="(0.465,0.997)" tail="(1.377,1.311)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="gp-vel" v="(-0.997,0.465)" tail="(1.377,1.311)"
        stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<point at="gp-P" p="(1.377,1.311)" fill="black" alignment="se">
<m>P</m>
</point>
<label at="gp-grad-label" anchor="(1.95,2.4)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
<m>\nabla f</m>
</label>
<label at="gp-vel-label" anchor="(0.3,1.85)" alignment="nw" color="#0072B2">
<m>\mathbf v(t)</m>
</label>
<label at="gp-C-label" anchor="(0,-1.6)" alignment="c" clear-background="yes" color="#009E73">
<m>C\colon f=c</m>
</label>
<label at="gp-outer-label" anchor="(0,-2.08)" alignment="c" clear-background="yes">
<m>f=c+\Delta c</m>
</label>
<label at="gp-inner-label" anchor="(0,-1.09)" alignment="c" clear-background="yes">
<m>f=c-\Delta c</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Three nested oval level curves of f. The middle green oval is the level curve C where f equals c. At a point P on C, a blue arrow is the velocity vector v of t, tangent to C, and a red arrow is the gradient of f, perpendicular to the velocity and pointing outward toward the larger level curve. A small square at P marks the right angle between them.">
  <annotation ref="gp-C" text="The level curve C, where f equals c, drawn in green."/>
  <annotation ref="gp-P" text="A point P on the level curve C."/>
  <annotation ref="gp-vel" text="The velocity vector v of t, drawn in blue, tangent to the level curve and pointing in the direction of motion along it."/>
  <annotation ref="gp-grad" text="The gradient vector of f, drawn in red, perpendicular to the velocity vector and pointing outward toward the level curve of larger value."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<activity xml:id="activity-gradient-perp-check">
<title>Checking that the Gradient Meets Level Curves at Right Angles</title>

<introduction>
<p>
<xref ref="thm-gradient-perp-level-curve"/> claims that <m>\nabla f</m> is
perpendicular to the level curve through a point. This activity asks you to verify
that claim by hand at one point, and then to test it at many points using the
interactive figure that follows.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="task-perp-level-curve">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>. Find the level curve of <m>f</m> through
<m>P_0(1,1)</m>, and give a vector tangent to that curve at <m>P_0</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<hint>
<p>
Level curves of this <m>f</m> are circles centred at the origin. A tangent to a
circle is perpendicular to its radius.
</p>
</hint>
<answer>
<p>
Since <m>f(1,1) = 3</m>, the level curve is <m>1 + x^2 + y^2 = 3</m>, that is the
circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 2</m> of radius <m>\sqrt 2</m>. The radius at <m>P_0</m>
points along <m>\hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath}</m>, so a tangent vector is
<m>\mathbf T = -\hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath}</m>.
</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-perp-dot-product">
<statement>
<p>
Compute <m>\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}</m> and then the dot product of that
gradient with your tangent vector. What does the result say?
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
<m>\nabla f = 2x\,\hat{\imath} + 2y\,\hat{\jmath}</m> gives
<m>\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)} = 2\,\hat{\imath} + 2\,\hat{\jmath}</m>, and
<md>
<mrow>
\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)} \cdot \mathbf T
\amp= (2)(-1) + (2)(1) = 0 .
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
A zero dot product between non-zero vectors means the two are orthogonal, so
<m>\nabla f</m> meets the level curve at a right angle at <m>P_0</m>, exactly as
<xref ref="thm-gradient-perp-level-curve"/> asserts.
</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-perp-general-point">
<statement>
<p>
Repeat the argument at an arbitrary point <m>(a,b) \ne (0,0)</m> to show the right
angle is not a coincidence of the point <m>(1,1)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
The level curve through <m>(a,b)</m> is the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = a^2 + b^2</m>,
whose tangent there is <m>\mathbf T = -b\,\hat{\imath} + a\,\hat{\jmath}</m>. Since
<m>\left(\nabla f\right)_{(a,b)} = 2a\,\hat{\imath} + 2b\,\hat{\jmath}</m>,
<md>
<mrow>
\left(\nabla f\right)_{(a,b)} \cdot \mathbf T
\amp= 2a(-b) + 2b(a) = 0 ,
</mrow>
</md>
so the gradient is perpendicular to the level curve at every point other than the
origin, where <m>\nabla f = \mathbf 0</m> and no direction is singled out.
</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-perp-interactive">
<statement>
<p>
Now drag the red point in <xref ref="fig-int-gradient-level-curves"/> to several locations,
including points on the axes and points far from the origin. Confirm that the
gradient arrow always meets the level curve at a right angle, and describe how the
length of the arrow changes as the point moves away from the origin. Can you place
the point where the gradient vanishes?
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-int-gradient-level-curves">
<caption>
The gradient <m>\nabla f</m> of <m>f(x,y)=1+x^2+y^2</m> at a movable point, shown with the level curve through that point, illustrating that <m>\nabla f</m> is perpendicular to the level curves.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-gradient-level-curves" iframe="gradient-level-curves.html" width="100%" aspect="7:6">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the red point anywhere in the plane. The gradient vector
<m>\nabla f = 2x\,\hat{\imath} + 2y\,\hat{\jmath}</m> of
<m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> is drawn at the point, together with the
level curve of <m>f</m> through the point. Observe that the gradient is
always perpendicular to the level curve and points in the direction in
which <m>f</m> increases.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
The right angle persists everywhere. The arrow lengthens as the point moves
outward, since <m>\|\nabla f\| = 2\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}</m> grows with the distance from
the origin; correspondingly the level curves are spaced more closely there, because
<m>f</m> is climbing faster. At the origin <m>\nabla f = \mathbf 0</m>: the level
curve degenerates to a single point and there is no direction of steepest ascent.
</p>
</answer>
</task>

</activity>

<p>
With <xref ref="eq-dirderiv-gradient-relation"/> in hand, the limit that defined
the directional derivative can be set aside: the gradient and a dot product do
the same work in a couple of lines. To see the difference, we rework
<xref ref="example-dirderiv-definition"/> <mdash/> the same function, point, and
direction <mdash/> and then read the answer geometrically off the surface.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-dirderiv-gradient">
<title>Example II</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>, the point
<m>P_0(1,1)</m>, and the unit vector
<m>\hat{u} = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\imath} + \dfrac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\jmath}</m>.
Evaluate the directional derivative
<m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m> and interpret your result.
</p>
</statement>


<solution>
<p>
We use
<m>\left(\dfrac{df}{ds}\right)_{\hat{u},P_0}
= \left(\nabla f\right)_{P_0}\cdot\hat{u}</m>.
First we compute the gradient vector <xref ref="eq-gradient-definition"/>:
<md>
<mrow>\nabla f \amp= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\,\hat{\imath}
        + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\,\hat{\jmath}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= (2x)\,\hat{\imath} + (2y)\,\hat{\jmath},</mrow>
<mrow>\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}
        \amp= \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)_{(1,1)}\hat{\imath}
        + \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)_{(1,1)}\hat{\jmath}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= 2\,\hat{\imath} + 2\,\hat{\jmath}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Then we take the dot product with <m>\hat{u}</m>:
<md>
<mrow>\left(\frac{df}{ds}\right)_{\hat{u},P_0} \amp= \left(\nabla f\right)_{P_0}\cdot\hat{u}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \left(2\,\hat{\imath} + 2\,\hat{\jmath}\right)\cdot
        \left(\tfrac{1}{\sqrt 2}\,\hat{\imath} + \tfrac{1}{\sqrt 2}\,\hat{\jmath}\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \frac{4}{\sqrt 2}</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-gradient-example-dot" number="yes">\amp= 2\sqrt 2,</mrow>
</md>
which agrees with the limit computation in
<xref ref="example-dirderiv-definition"/>. See
<xref ref="fig-dirderiv-surface-tikz"/> for a geometrical interpretation
of the directional derivative. Note that the unit vector
<m>\hat{u} = \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\imath} + \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\jmath}</m>
is the direction vector of the line <m>y = x</m> located in the
<m>xy</m>-plane. The plane <m>x - y = 0</m> contains this line and is
orthogonal to the <m>xy</m>-plane. The intersection of the surface
<m>z = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> and the plane <m>x - y = 0</m> is the parabola
shown in the figure. Finally, the directional derivative of
<m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> at the point <m>(1,1)</m> corresponds to
the slope of the tangent line to the surface <m>z = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> at
<m>(1,1,3)</m> that is located in the plane <m>x - y = 0</m>.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-dirderiv-surface-tikz">
<caption>
The surface <m>z = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> cut by the vertical plane
<m>x - y = 0</m>, which contains the direction <m>\hat{u}</m>. The
intersection is a parabola, and the directional derivative
<m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{(1,1)} = 2\sqrt 2</m> is the slope of
its tangent line at the point <m>(1,1,3)</m>. The level curve
<m>f = 3</m> through <m>(1,1)</m>, the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 2</m>, is
drawn on the surface at height <m>z = 3</m>, with its dashed projection
in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-dirderiv-surface-tikz" width="70%">
<shortdescription>
A paraboloid cut by a vertical plane, with the trace parabola and its
tangent line at the point one comma one comma three.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
The surface <m>z = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>, an upward-opening paraboloid,
drawn as a translucent blue surface. A translucent yellow vertical
plane <m>x - y = 0</m> passes through the surface above the line
<m>y = x</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane, where the unit vector
<m>\hat{u}</m> is drawn as a blue arrow starting at <m>(1,1,0)</m>.
The plane meets the surface in a parabola, drawn in solid vermillion,
and a green tangent line touches this parabola at the marked point
<m>(1,1,3)</m>. The slope of this tangent line, measured within the
vertical plane, is the directional derivative <m>2\sqrt 2</m>. A
reddish-purple circle on the surface at height <m>z = 3</m> marks the
level curve <m>f = 3</m>, and its dashed projection in the
<m>xy</m>-plane is the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 2</m> through the point
<m>(1,1)</m>.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
  \definecolor{oiyellow}{RGB}{240,228,66}
  \definecolor{oipurple}{RGB}{204,121,167}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={115}{18},
      width=11.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=-2.1, xmax=2.1, ymin=-2.1, ymax=2.1, zmin=0, zmax=4.9,
      clip=false,
      legend style={
        at={(0.5,1.04)}, anchor=south,
        legend columns=2,
        font=\scriptsize,
        draw=black!35, rounded corners=2pt,
        fill=white, fill opacity=0.92, text opacity=1,
        inner sep=4pt, row sep=2pt, column sep=10pt,
      },
      legend cell align=left,
      legend image post style={line width=1.4pt, sharp plot},
    ]
    % ---- vertical plane x - y = 0 (drawn first, behind surface) ----
    \fill[oiyellow, opacity=0.28]
      (axis cs:-1.7,-1.7,0) -- (axis cs:1.7,1.7,0)
      -- (axis cs:1.7,1.7,4.4) -- (axis cs:-1.7,-1.7,4.4) -- cycle;
    % ---- surface: paraboloid z = 1 + x^2 + y^2 ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=interp,
              point meta={z},
              colormap={softz}{color=(oiblue!12!white) color=(oiblue!38!white)},
              opacity=0.5, fill opacity=0.5,
              domain=0:360, samples=61,
              y domain=0:1.7, samples y=25,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({y*cos(x)}, {y*sin(x)}, {1 + y^2});
    % surface listed in the legend (so the equation never overlaps the plot)
    \addlegendimage{area legend, fill=oiblue!30, draw=oiblue!45}
    \addlegendentry{surface $z=1+x^2+y^2$}
    % light parallels (circles) for shape cues
    \foreach \rr in {0.5,0.9,1.3,1.7}{
      \edef\tmp{\noexpand\addplot3[black!22, line width=0.35pt, domain=0:360,
          samples=97, samples y=0, forget plot]
        ({\rr*cos(x)}, {\rr*sin(x)}, {1 + \rr*\rr});}\tmp}
    % ---- trace: parabola z = 1 + 2t^2 along x = y = t ----
    \addplot3[line width=1.7pt, oivermillion, domain=-1.2:1.2,
        samples=61, samples y=0]
      (x, x, {1 + 2*x^2});
    \addlegendentry{trace in the plane $x-y=0$}
    % ---- tangent line at (1,1,3) ----
    \addplot3[line width=1.7pt, oigreen, domain=-0.55:0.55,
        samples=2, samples y=0]
      ({1 + x/sqrt(2)}, {1 + x/sqrt(2)}, {3 + 2*sqrt(2)*x});
    \addlegendentry{tangent line, slope $2\sqrt2$}
    % ---- level curve f = 3 through (1,1): x^2 + y^2 = 2 ----
    \addplot3[line width=1.6pt, oipurple, domain=0:360, samples=121,
        samples y=0]
      ({sqrt(2)*cos(x)}, {sqrt(2)*sin(x)}, 3);
    \addlegendentry{level curve $x^2+y^2=2$}
    \addplot3[line width=1.2pt, oipurple, dashed, domain=0:360, samples=121,
        samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({sqrt(2)*cos(x)}, {sqrt(2)*sin(x)}, 0);
    % ---- dashed drop line and marked point ----
    \addplot3[black!60, dashed, line width=0.6pt, samples y=0, forget plot]
      coordinates {(1,1,0) (1,1,3)};
    \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8pt, color=black, forget plot]
      coordinates {(1,1,3)};
    \node[anchor=west, font=\small] at (axis cs:1.1,1.1,3.3) {$(1,1,3)$};
    % ---- unit vector u in the xy-plane ----
    \draw[-{Latex}, oiblue, line width=1.5pt]
      (axis cs:1,1,0) -- (axis cs:1.5,1.5,0);
    \node[oiblue, anchor=north west, font=\small] at (axis cs:1.45,1.45,0)
      {$\hat{u}=\left\langle\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2},\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle$};
    \node[anchor=south east, font=\small] at (axis cs:1,1,-0.12) {$(1,1)$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
