</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-dirderiv-properties">
<title>Directions of Fastest Increase, Fastest Decrease, and No Change</title>

<p>
The directional derivative can be expressed as
<md>
<mrow>D_{\hat{u}} f \amp= \nabla f\cdot\hat{u}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \|\nabla f\|\,\|\hat{u}\|\cos\theta</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-dirderiv-cos-theta" number="yes">\amp= \|\nabla f\|\cos\theta,</mrow>
</md>
where <m>\theta</m> is the angle between <m>\nabla f</m> and the unit
vector <m>\hat{u}</m>. The first line is
<xref ref="eq-dirderiv-gradient-relation"/>, obtained from the chain rule in
<xref ref="sec-gradient-vector"/>; the second is the geometric form of the dot
product; and the third uses <m>\|\hat{u}\| = 1</m>.
</p>

<remark xml:id="remark-dirderiv-three-consequences">
<title>Three Consequences of <m>D_{\hat{u}} f = \|\nabla f\|\cos\theta</m></title>
<p>
Only the factor <m>\cos\theta</m> depends on the direction, and it runs over
<m>[-1,1]</m> as <m>\hat{u}</m> turns through the circle of directions at
<m>P_0</m>. Its three extreme values single out three directions, drawn in
<xref ref="fig-dirderiv-angle-prefigure"/>:
</p>
<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>
When <m>\nabla f</m> and <m>\hat{u}</m> are in the same direction
(<m>\cos\theta = 1</m>), <m>f</m> increases most rapidly and
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f = \|\nabla f\|</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
When <m>\nabla f</m> and <m>\hat{u}</m> have opposite directions
(<m>\cos\theta = -1</m>), <m>f</m> decreases most rapidly and
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f = -\|\nabla f\|</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
When <m>\nabla f</m> and <m>\hat{u}</m> are orthogonal
(<m>\cos\theta = 0</m>), <m>f</m> does not change, and
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f = 0</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</remark>

<figure xml:id="fig-dirderiv-angle-prefigure">
<caption>
Directions around <m>P_0</m>: <m>f</m> increases fastest along
<m>\nabla f</m>, decreases fastest along <m>-\nabla f</m>, and does not
change in the two directions orthogonal to <m>\nabla f</m>.
</caption>
<image width="52%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-dirderiv-angle">
<diagram dimensions="(320,320)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.9,-1.9,1.9,1.9)">
<circle at="an-circle" center="(0,0)" radius="1" stroke="#7F7F7F"
        dash="6 4" fill="none"/>
<vector at="an-grad" v="(1.405,0.655)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#D55E00"
        thickness="3"/>
<vector at="an-u" v="(0.087,0.996)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2"
        thickness="3"/>
<vector at="an-zero1" v="(-0.423,0.906)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#7F7F7F"
        thickness="2"/>
<vector at="an-zero2" v="(0.423,-0.906)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#7F7F7F"
        thickness="2"/>
<point at="an-P0" p="(0,0)" fill="black" alignment="sw">
<m>P_0</m>
</point>
<label at="an-grad-label" anchor="(1.5,0.8)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
<m>\nabla f</m>
</label>
<label at="an-u-label" anchor="(0.09,1.1)" alignment="n" color="#0072B2">
<m>\hat{u}</m>
</label>
<label at="an-zero1-label" anchor="(-0.55,1.15)" alignment="nw">
<m>D_{\hat{u}}f=0</m>
</label>
<label at="an-zero2-label" anchor="(0.55,-1.15)" alignment="se">
<m>D_{\hat{u}}f=0</m>
</label>
<label at="an-formula" anchor="(0,-1.62)" alignment="c">
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f = \|\nabla f\|\cos\theta</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A point P 0 surrounded by a dashed unit circle of possible directions. A long red arrow shows the gradient of f. A blue unit arrow shows a direction u making an angle theta with the gradient. Two gray arrows orthogonal to the gradient mark the directions of zero change. Below the circle is the formula: the directional derivative equals the magnitude of the gradient times cosine theta.">
  <annotation ref="an-grad" text="The gradient vector of f at P 0, drawn in red. Moving in this direction gives the most rapid increase, with directional derivative equal to the magnitude of the gradient."/>
  <annotation ref="an-u" text="A unit vector u, drawn in blue, making an angle theta with the gradient. Its directional derivative is the magnitude of the gradient times cosine theta."/>
  <annotation ref="an-zero1" text="A direction orthogonal to the gradient, where cosine theta is zero, so the directional derivative is zero and f does not change."/>
  <annotation ref="an-zero2" text="The opposite orthogonal direction, where the directional derivative is also zero."/>
  <annotation ref="an-circle" text="The dashed unit circle of all possible directions u at the point P 0."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<activity xml:id="activity-verify-cos-theta">
<title>Verifying the Three Special Directions</title>

<introduction>
<p>
The three cases listed above are all consequences of a single formula,
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f = \|\nabla f\|\cos\theta</m>. In this activity you will derive them
yourself and then check your answers against
<xref ref="fig-int-gradient-direction-angle"/>, where <m>\hat{u}</m> can be dragged
around the circle of directions.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="task-cos-theta-formula">
<statement>
<p>
Beginning with <m>D_{\hat{u}} f = \nabla f \cdot \hat{u}</m>, explain why the dot
product can be rewritten as <m>\|\nabla f\|\cos\theta</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
For any two vectors, <m>\mathbf a \cdot \mathbf b = \|\mathbf a\|\|\mathbf b\|\cos\theta</m>
with <m>\theta</m> the angle between them. Applying this to <m>\nabla f</m> and
<m>\hat{u}</m>,
<md>
<mrow>D_{\hat{u}} f = \nabla f \cdot \hat{u}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \|\nabla f\|\,\|\hat{u}\|\cos\theta</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \|\nabla f\|\cos\theta ,</mrow>
</md>
where the last step uses <m>\|\hat{u}\| = 1</m>. Without that assumption the factor
<m>\|\hat{u}\|</m> would survive, and rescaling <m>\hat{u}</m> would change
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f</m> without changing the direction being measured.
</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cos-theta-extremes">
<statement>
<p>
Treat <m>\|\nabla f\|</m> as fixed and let <m>\theta</m> run over
<m>[0, 2\pi)</m>. What are the largest and smallest values of
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f</m>, and at which angles do they occur? For which angles is
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f = 0</m>?
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
Since <m>-1 \le \cos\theta \le 1</m>, the quantity <m>\|\nabla f\|\cos\theta</m> ranges
between <m>-\|\nabla f\|</m> and <m>\|\nabla f\|</m>. The largest value <m>\|\nabla f\|</m>
occurs at <m>\theta = 0</m>, with <m>\hat{u}</m> pointing along <m>\nabla f</m>; the
smallest, <m>-\|\nabla f\|</m>, occurs at <m>\theta = \pi</m>, with <m>\hat{u}</m>
pointing along <m>-\nabla f</m>. The directional derivative vanishes when
<m>\cos\theta = 0</m>, that is at <m>\theta = \pi/2</m> and <m>\theta = 3\pi/2</m>,
the two directions orthogonal to <m>\nabla f</m>.
</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cos-theta-concrete">
<statement>
<p>
Now take <m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> at <m>P_0(1,1)</m>, the function of
<xref ref="example-dirderiv-definition"/>. Compute <m>\nabla f</m> and
<m>\|\nabla f\|</m> at <m>P_0</m>, write <m>D_{\hat{u}} f</m> as a function of
<m>\theta</m>, and give the three special unit vectors explicitly.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
Here <m>\nabla f = 2x\,\hat{\imath} + 2y\,\hat{\jmath}</m>, so
<m>(\nabla f)_{(1,1)} = 2\,\hat{\imath} + 2\,\hat{\jmath}</m> and
<m>\|\nabla f\| = 2\sqrt 2</m>. Therefore
<md>
<mrow>
D_{\hat{u}} f = 2\sqrt 2 \,\cos\theta .
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
The fastest increase happens along
<m>\hat{u} = \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\imath} + \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\jmath}</m>, giving
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f = 2\sqrt 2 \approx 2.83</m>, which is the value computed in
<xref ref="example-dirderiv-definition"/>. The fastest decrease happens along
<m>-\frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\imath} - \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\jmath}</m>, giving
<m>-2\sqrt 2</m>. There is no change along
<m>\pm\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\imath} - \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\jmath}\right)</m>, the two
directions orthogonal to <m>\nabla f</m>.
</p>
</answer>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cos-theta-check">
<statement>
<p>
Check your work against the figure below. Drag
<m>\hat{u}</m> around the circle and confirm that the meter agrees with
<m>2\sqrt 2\cos\theta</m> at several angles. Then use the three buttons and compare the
readings with your answers to the previous task. As you drag, at what angle does the
reading change most quickly, and at what angle is it momentarily stationary?
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-int-gradient-direction-angle">
<caption>
The directional derivative <m>D_{\hat{u}} f = \|\nabla f\|\cos\theta</m> as the unit vector <m>\hat{u}</m> rotates around <m>P_0</m>.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-gradient-direction-angle" iframe="gradient-direction-angle.html" width="100%" aspect="7:6">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the blue unit vector <m>\hat{u}</m> around the circle of
directions and watch the meter display
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f = \|\nabla f\|\cos\theta</m>. Use the buttons to snap
<m>\hat{u}</m> to the three special cases: the same direction as
<m>\nabla f</m> (fastest increase), the opposite direction (fastest
decrease), and a direction orthogonal to <m>\nabla f</m> (no change).
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
The three buttons should give <m>2\sqrt 2 \approx 2.83</m>,
<m>-2\sqrt 2 \approx -2.83</m>, and <m>0</m>. The reading changes fastest where
<m>\left|\frac{d}{d\theta}\cos\theta\right| = |\sin\theta|</m> is largest, namely at
<m>\theta = \pi/2</m> and <m>3\pi/2</m>, the very directions where
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f</m> itself is zero. It is momentarily stationary at
<m>\theta = 0</m> and <m>\theta = \pi</m>, where <m>D_{\hat{u}} f</m> is largest and
smallest. So a small change of heading matters least when you are already pointing
straight uphill, and most when you are moving along a level direction.
</p>
</answer>
</task>

</activity>


<example xml:id="example-gradient-directions">
<title>Example III</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function
<m>f(x,y) = \dfrac{x^2}{2} + \dfrac{y^2}{2}</m>. Find the directions in
which, at the point <m>P_0(1,1)</m>,
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>f</m> increases most rapidly,
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>f</m> decreases most rapidly,
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
there is no change in <m>f</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>


<solution>
<p>
<em>A.</em> As explained above, <m>f</m> increases most rapidly in the
direction of <m>\nabla f</m>. We have
<md>
<mrow>\nabla f \amp= x\,\hat{\imath} + y\,\hat{\jmath} \quad\Rightarrow\quad \left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-directions-gradient" number="yes">\amp= \hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Note that we need to divide
<m>\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)} = \hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath}</m> by its
length to find the unit vector corresponding to the direction of most
rapid increase:
<md xml:id="eq-directions-increase" number="yes">
\frac{\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}}{\left\|\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}\right\|}
= \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\imath} + \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\jmath}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<em>B.</em> For the direction of most rapid decrease, we take the
opposite direction:
<md xml:id="eq-directions-decrease" number="yes">
-\frac{\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}}{\left\|\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}\right\|}
= -\frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\imath} - \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\jmath}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<em>C.</em> To find the directions of zero change in <m>f</m>, we require
<md>
<mrow>\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}\cdot\hat{u} \amp= (\hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath})\cdot(u_1\hat{\imath} + u_2\hat{\jmath})</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= u_1 + u_2</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= 0 \quad\text{or}\quad u_1</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-directions-zero-dot" number="yes">\amp= -u_2,</mrow>
</md>
and since <m>\hat{u}</m> is a unit vector, we have
<md xml:id="eq-directions-unit-length" number="yes">
\|\hat{u}\|^2 = u_1^2 + u_2^2 = 1.
</md>
</p>

<p>
After substituting <xref ref="eq-directions-zero-dot"/> in
<xref ref="eq-directions-unit-length"/>, we conclude
<md xml:id="eq-directions-zero-solve" number="yes">
2u_1^2 = 1 \quad\Rightarrow\quad u_1 = \pm\frac{1}{\sqrt 2}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Hence, the directions of zero change in <m>f</m> at <m>P_0(1,1)</m> are
<md>
<mrow>\hat{u} \amp= \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\imath} - \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\jmath} \qquad\text{and}\qquad \tilde{\hat{u}}</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-directions-zero-answer" number="yes">\amp= -\frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\imath} + \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\jmath}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Note that <m>\tilde{\hat{u}} = -\hat{u}</m>. All four directions are
shown in <xref ref="fig-gradient-directions-prefigure"/>: the directions of zero
change are tangent to the level curve of <m>f</m> through <m>P_0(1,1)</m>,
while the directions of most rapid increase and decrease are
perpendicular to it.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-gradient-directions-prefigure">
<caption>
At <m>P_0(1,1)</m>: fastest increase along
<m>\nabla f/\|\nabla f\|</m>, fastest decrease along
<m>-\nabla f/\|\nabla f\|</m>, and zero change along
<m>\pm\hat{u}</m>, tangent to the level curve <m>f = 1</m>.
</caption>
<image width="52%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-gradient-directions">
<diagram dimensions="(320,320)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(-2.5,-2.5,2.7,2.7)">
<axes at="gd-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<circle at="gd-level1" center="(0,0)" radius="0.707" stroke="#7F7F7F"
        fill="none"/>
