<m>f = 1</m>, the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 2</m>, passing through the point
<m>(1,1,1)</m>; its dashed projection lies in the <m>xy</m>-plane. Drag
the slider to rotate the direction
<m>\hat{u} = \langle\cos\varphi, \sin\varphi\rangle</m> at
<m>P_0(1,1)</m>: the vertical plane in the direction <m>\hat{u}</m>
cuts the surface in a curve whose tangent line at <m>(1,1,1)</m> has
slope <m>D_{\hat{u}} f = \cos\varphi + \sin\varphi</m>. Use the
buttons to snap to the answers of Example III, and note that when
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f = 0</m>, the vector <m>\hat{u}</m> is tangent to
