<figure xml:id="fig-int-gradient-max-directions-3d-manim">
<caption>
The same 3D view, animated: the tangent to the sliced surface at
<m>(1,1,1)</m> tipping from steepest ascent through level to steepest
descent as <m>\hat{u}</m> turns.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-gradient-max-directions-3d-manim" iframe="gradient-max-directions-3d-manim.html" width="100%" aspect="1:1">
<instructions>
<p>
Press <q>Play</q> to watch the vertical plane through <m>P_0(1,1)</m>
turn once around. The gold curve is where it cuts the surface
<m>z = \frac{x^2}{2} + \frac{y^2}{2}</m>, and the straight line through
<m>(1,1,1)</m> is the tangent to that curve, whose slope is
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f</m>: it is green and steepest upwards along
<m>\nabla f</m> (A), red and steepest downwards along <m>-\nabla f</m>
(B), and yellow and level in the two directions tangent to the pink
level curve <m>f = 1</m> (C). Stop the animation at any time and drag
the <m>\varphi</m> slider, drag the figure to change the viewpoint, or
press <q>Spin</q>.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-gradient-summary">
<title>Summary</title>

<p>
We collect here the main ideas of this section.
</p>

<figure xml:id="video-dirderiv-2d-3d">
<caption>
A directional derivative viewed together in the plane and in space: the unit
vector <m>\hat{u}</m> at <m>P_0</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane selects a slice of
the surface <m>z=f(x,y)</m>, and <m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m> is the
slope of the resulting tangent line to that slice.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-dirderiv-definition-2d-3d"
       source="videos/dirderiv_definition_2D_3D.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/dirderiv_definition_2D_3D.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<em>The directional derivative.</em>
<xref ref="def-directional-derivative"/> defines
<m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m> as the limit in
<xref ref="eq-dirderiv-definition"/>; it measures the rate of change of
<m>f</m> at <m>P_0</m> in the direction of a unit vector <m>\hat{u}</m>.
It is computed directly from this limit in
<xref ref="example-dirderiv-definition"/>, and
<xref ref="video-dirderiv-2d-3d"/> shows how the plane and space pictures fit
together.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<em>The gradient vector.</em> Using the chain rule we obtain
<xref ref="def-gradient-vector"/>, which gives the shortcut
<m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}
= \left(\nabla f\right)_{P_0}\cdot\hat{u}</m> in
<xref ref="eq-dirderiv-gradient-relation"/>. This is applied in
<xref ref="example-dirderiv-gradient"/>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<em>Fastest increase, fastest decrease, and no change.</em> Writing
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f = \|\nabla f\|\cos\theta</m> in
<xref ref="eq-dirderiv-cos-theta"/> shows that <m>f</m> increases most
rapidly in the direction of <m>\nabla f</m>, decreases most rapidly in
the direction of <m>-\nabla f</m>, and does not change in the directions
orthogonal to <m>\nabla f</m>. These directions are found in
<xref ref="example-gradient-directions"/>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<em>The gradient is perpendicular to the level curves.</em>
<xref ref="thm-gradient-perp-level-curve"/> proves that <m>\nabla f</m> is
perpendicular to the level curve through each point. Equivalently, the
directions of no change are tangent to the level curve, while the
directions of most rapid increase and decrease are perpendicular to it.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

</subsection>

<xi:include href="subsec-scalar-fields-potentials.ptx"/>

</section>
